; Entity templates: the initial state of objects that are created while the game runs
;
; Each line is the template of one kind of object, a stack frame in the format of the object slots (#R$C100). #R$8905
; copies a template to a free slot when an entry of the object list (#R$BE56: template address, y, x) is consumed:
; bytes 0-5 (three words) as they are, then slot +6 = 0 and slot +7 = x + template byte 6, slot +8 = 0 and slot +9 = y
; + template byte 7 (the two offsets are signed, in 2-pixel units, and make the start position relative to the object
; that threw the thing), then the 10 bytes from template byte 8 to slot +$A..+$13 as they are. The words of a template
; are therefore: +0 the handler, +2 and +4 the first two state words of the handler (for the projectiles the x and y
; speed, signed 8.8 in 2-pixel units per frame), +6 the two offsets, +8 hit box offset (y, x), +$A hit box size (y,
; x), then the pointer to the behaviour block with the sprite description (attribute, pair table, height, frames; see
; #R$93D3) or, for the handlers that keep the animation words in other places, those. A template of 8 words is
; followed in memory by the next template: the 10-byte copy takes two bytes too many from it and they land in slot
; +$12/+$13, which the projectile handlers do not use. Slot +$14/+$15 are left as they were.
; .
; The last template of the group (#R$BD6A) is not spawned this way: #R$BB57 copies its 22 bytes over its own slot. The
; templates from #R$BD80 on belong to the actors of the ending (the lists at #R$83A8).
@label=ArrowLeftTemplate
b$BC92 DEFW $B6ED,$FE00,$0000,$0600,$0000,$0400,$0202,$94F9 ; arrow flying left ($FE00 = -2.0 per frame), thrown by an archer when the monk is to its left (#R$B5EB); handler #R$B6ED
@label=ArrowRightTemplate
 $BCA2 DEFW $B6ED,$0200,$0000,$0603,$0000,$0400,$0202,$94FC ; arrow flying right ($0200), same archer when the monk is to its right
@label=WallShotTemplate
